<%# One arriving order, announced on whichever page of the dashboard the owner
    happens to be on. It is the shared flash band and nothing else — the same
    ground, rule, label and dismiss control every other message in this app
    uses — because a notification that looked like its own kind of object would
    be a second vocabulary for the same job.

    `variant` is passed as the whole class name, never assembled: Tailwind
    builds its stylesheet by scanning these files for literal class strings, so
    a name stitched together at render time gets no rule emitted at all and the
    band silently loses its ground.

    The message is a link rather than a sentence with a link after it. The one
    thing the owner wants from this band is the order, and role="alert" on the
    band announces the copy without pulling focus into it, so the link is
    reachable but never grabbed mid-task. %>
<%= render "shared/flash_band",
           variant: "alert-info",
           icon_name: "bell-ringing",
           label: t("owner.orders.new_order"),
           message: link_to(
             t("owner.orders.new_order_detail",
               id: order.id,
               table: order.restaurant_table&.name || t("owner.orders.no_table")),
             owner_restaurant_order_path(restaurant, order),
             class: "underline underline-offset-2"
           ) %>
